NOTE FOR THE PROJECT OWNER
--------------------------
This is a placeholder so the repository is not published without any licence terms at
all, which would leave its status ambiguous. Replace it before distributing, and
decide two things while you do:

  1. The commercial terms for the product itself.

  2. The terms for `bin/vault-verify.js`. The product's proof story depends on that
     file being independently auditable — the specification calls for a standalone
     open-source verifier, so that an auditor can check the ledger without trusting
     either the vendor or the operator. A permissive licence on that one file
     (Apache-2.0 or MIT) supports that claim; keeping it proprietary undercuts it.
     It imports nothing from src/ specifically so it can be carved out and published
     on its own.

